{"kind":"artifact","text":"Successor artifact: the deletion-style merge-plan staleness regression, rebased onto codex 5f3734fb as planner asked at 9975, at exact clean pushed head 9ff932bdc953698bc160e927675aa48bb3d000de on request/merge-plan-staleness-regression. This replaces my 09068b17, which stood at dafae40f cut from main.\n\nThe rebase applied cleanly with no conflict. I verified at the head rather than taking the rebase on trust: codex TestMergeSuccessorThroughIntermediateProvenanceStaysCurrent is byte-identical to their copy at 5f3734fb, my TestDeletionMergeReceiptOutlivesTheStalenessItsOwnPlanCauses is present in full, git diff --stat 5f3734fb..HEAD is one file and 56 insertions with zero deletions, and the Rests-On trailer still resolves to request 0a1875406ff4cf3925425d2f2026c4ff70acf086.\n\nBoth directions re-run at this head rather than carried over from dafae40f, and I reproduced the mutation myself rather than relaying it. Rule intact: whole internal/workroom package ok, both tests pass. stalenessCoveredByMergePlan neutered with return false as its first statement, go build exit 0 so it is a real measurement: exactly two failures in the package, and they are the two stacked new tests --\n\n  TestMergeSuccessorThroughIntermediateProvenanceStaysCurrent, fold_test.go:2244, successor resting on intermediate provenance: retired=false stale=true world=true\n  TestDeletionMergeReceiptOutlivesTheStalenessItsOwnPlanCauses, fold_test.go:2335, the receipt flared over the retirement its own plan names\n\nplanner predicted at 9975 that stacking would make one mutation kill both, and that is what happens. It is a real limit of the pair, not something the test can be written around: the two guard one condition from opposite directions, so what distinguishes them is the construction and the failure message, not the mutation that kills them.\n\nThe direct-basis exemption at fold.go:1629 was neutered separately, build exit 0, and fails five sibling tests by name while